{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "Every autumn the lighthouse keeper's daughter, Mara, climbed the spiral stairs to help her father prepare the great lamp for the stormy months ahead. There were one hundred and twelve steps, and she had counted them so many times that her feet knew the rhythm without thinking. At the top, the lantern room smelled of salt, brass polish, and the faint sweetness of lamp oil. Her father handed her a soft cloth, and together they cleaned each prism of the enormous lens until it gleamed like a cluster of frozen waterfalls. Through the tall windows the sea stretched grey and restless in every direction, broken only by the white backs of waves and the occasional dark shape of a fishing boat heading home. Her father told her stories while they worked: of the winter the ice closed the harbor, of the night a ship's captain rowed ashore just to thank the keeper for guiding him past the rocks, and of Mara's grandmother, who once kept the light burning alone for three days during a gale. Mara listened and polished, polished and listened. When the sun began to sink, her father let her strike the match. The flame caught, grew steady, and the lens gathered it into a single brilliant beam that swept out across the darkening water. Mara pressed her face to the glass and watched the light travel farther than she could see. Somewhere out there, she thought, someone was looking toward the shore, and because of this small flame, they would know the way home."}
